Z||000000||Cheever3000||10-22-2001||07:52 PM||||<B>I keep seeing stuff like this (from Yahoo's news)...</B><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>"The president of neighboring Pakistan, Gen. Pervez Musharraf, said he hoped military operations in Afghanistan would be over by mid-November, when the Muslim holy month of Ramadan begins. Leaders throughout the Muslim world fear a backlash if operations continue against Muslim Afghanistan during Ramadan. <BR>While saying the U.S.-led campaign should continue until its objectives are met, Musharraf said bombing during Ramadan    <P>``would certainly have some negative effects in the Muslim world.'' During Ramadan, Muslims fast from sunrise to sunset. <P>``So one would hope and wish that this campaign comes to an end before the month of Ramadan, and one would hope for restraint during the month of Ramadan,'' he said on CNN's ``Larry King Live.''<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B>Why is it important to them that we show any mercy on the Taliban outlaws, just because of the Muslim holidays?  If they denounce them as not being true keepers of their faith, then why the heck do they keep defending them?  I say if they still haven't been wiped out by then, don't stop going after em!!!  They must be killed, so what's the big woof whether we do it before or after <I>anybody's</I> holiday?</B><P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fformula88||10-22-2001||09:14 PM||||I'd say Pakistan has two motives here...<P>First they are friends of the Taliban... as much as one can be anyway.  Pakistan is only one of like two countries in the world that recognize the Taliban as the official government of Afghanistan and havediplomatic relations with them.<P>Second, there own a$$es are on the line.  The protestors in Pakistan get more and mroe violent, and if the war carries into Ramadan, they may go over the deep end and try to oust the Pakistani government for helping the Americans. <P>Don't forget, a lot of people in that area of the world are illiterate.  Their media is less than free to report what it wants and they are very religious and will believe what their religious leaders say.  Osama bin-Laden is a hero to a lot of Muslims.  Essentially they see us as the enemy for all these reasons, we are attacking their hero and they have no means to get the information they would need for a more rational decision.<P>The holiday is just an excuse for some of the less supportive muslim countries in the coalition to back out IMO.  I do not think the US is going to stop their actions for a month long holiday.  Its going to end when the mission has been accomplished.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||10-22-2001||09:46 PM||||I think the bombing (at least) will stop during Ramadan. This is one of the most important Holy times in the Islamic calendar.<BR>PC pretty well dictates that we respect this time. Don't mean I necessarily agree, but it's going to probably happen that way. During this month-long fast, nothing of any entertainment, other than prayers and visiting family & friends is allowed. Ramadan celebrates the deliverence of the Quoran to earth. It is also during this fast, that God decides the course of the world for the following year.||||reg||1
Z||000003||frontal lobe||10-22-2001||09:52 PM||||Well, living in the U.S. with the media the way it is, you would think the U.S. is the only country or people with actions based on racism and bigotry and not based on treating people based on their behavior.  So that's why you ask the question.  The answer is that kind of thinking is prevalent throughout the entire world, just we only here what the media wants heard.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||10-22-2001||10:41 PM||||Screw that. They killed 6000 innocent American civilians without *any* regard for *anything*, much less a frickin' holiday. <P>Bomb the MF's until the mission is completed. Screw their holiday. *They* (the Taliban) made the rules here, not us. They started it, and now we're going to finish it. ||||reg||1
