Z||000016||TRiAD||10-23-2001||08:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B> Well, you have to understand that most of the Muslim world sees the Taliban as "crusaders" for Islamic purity. In other words, they kinda stand for all that is good and right with Islam. Well, at least they WERE until the later 90s, AFTER they had established a solid foothold. That's when the attrocities started. And of course, the Taliban is run by a group of high-priests. So therefore, attacks on the Taliban would be similar to attacking the Vatican (for those of the Catholic persuasion)...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, no. The Taliban is NOT respected by the Islam nations. They are "a rogue dictatorial regime" or something like that. <BR>In other words, other Afghanistanis are glad we're about to rid them of this dictatorship, and let the "real leaders" (this is their priests) lead again. <P><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mach10||10-23-2001||09:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Uh, no. The Taliban is NOT respected by the Islam nations. They are "a rogue dictatorial regime" or something like that. <BR>In other words, other Afghanistanis are glad we're about to rid them of this dictatorship, and let the "real leaders" (this is their priests) lead again. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Nope, that's the opinion of the Afghanis... Even then, there's support for the Taliban within the country. Given the option between American/N. Alliance rule and Taliban rule, they'd still rather have the Taliban.<P>The reasons for the Taliban's success are still in place. The Taliban got into power because the offered a <I>relatively</I> peaceful solution to the brutal in-fighting and civil war. Of course, once they got into power, the former rival factions banded up and headed for the hills. What do you think will happen when the Taliban topples? The old government will come back and set everything right? Don't think so. They will be back to the way they were in the 80s, a blasted, war-torn country made up of military states. The power vacuum will open up, and so will the gunfire. And the Afghanis know this.<P>Aside from that, the taliban is held in high regard in many muslim countries, Pakistan having the largest complement of pro-Taliban movements. Thing is, though, that the only thing on people's minds is that the West (for the most part not particularly well-liked) are killing Muslims, and Muslim priests, and going after an International Hero (OSB).<P>||||reg||3
